{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "The cross section is a one-variable resonance curve sigma(E_cm) with a single shared line shape determined only by E_cm through the Breit-Wigner denominator and the existing energy-dependent width ansatz.",
      "child_relation": "The line shape becomes sigma(E_cm, beam_energy_spread), where the observed cross section is the resonance profile averaged over a finite collision-energy distribution. This introduces an interaction between the local resonance curvature and the beam-energy spread, changing the apparent peak height and shoulder behavior.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "E_cm"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed resonance parameters, scans taken with narrower versus broader effective collision-energy spread show different peak suppression and central flattening near E_cm ≈ resonance_mass, while far from resonance the curves approach each other."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "detuning_sq",
        "expression": "(E_cm**2 - resonance_mass**2)**2",
        "role": "distance from the resonance pole in the unsmeared propagator"
      },
      {
        "name": "scaled_energy",
        "expression": "E_cm/resonance_mass",
        "role": "dimensionless off-shell energy controlling the existing width relaxation"
      },
      {
        "name": "running_width_sq",
        "expression": "(intrinsic_width*(E_cm/resonance_mass)*(1 + width_relaxation_scale*((E_cm/resonance_mass)**2 - 1)))**2",
        "role": "local intrinsic broadening scale before detector/beam smearing"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when the machine collision-energy spread is not negligible compared with the intrinsic resonance width or with the energy step size near the peak, especially for |E_cm - resonance_mass| on the order of a few intrinsic widths.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "If training samples mostly cover off-peak points, the parent and child can look similar there, but holdout including dense points around the resonance apex reveals peak lowering and line-shape smoothing that extend the same smearing mechanism.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the zero-spread limit, where the collision-energy distribution collapses to a delta function at the nominal E_cm.",
    "freedom_change": "add_input",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "Finite beam-energy spread is an independent machine setting or run condition, not inferable from E_cm alone and not representable by a single global refit of existing resonance parameters when comparing scans taken under different spread conditions.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes the collision energy is known exactly for every event and therefore equates the measured cross section with the unsmeared resonance curve.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The collision energy is known exactly and detector acceptance is constant.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Finite accelerator beam-energy spread convolves the intrinsic resonance profile with an external energy distribution, modifying the measured cross section without changing the underlying resonance pole parameters.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "E_cm",
      "sigma_peak",
      "resonance_mass",
      "intrinsic_width",
      "width_relaxation_scale"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the exact-energy assumption rather than add an unrelated new process. The parent already models intrinsic width variation with energy, but it still maps each nominal E_cm to a single unsmeared cross section. In actual energy scans, the independently varied condition is the beam-energy spread (or equivalent machine resolution), while the underlying resonance parameters are held fixed. Under narrow-spread conditions the measured response closely follows the current sharp resonance profile; under broader-spread conditions the same intrinsic resonance is averaged over nearby energies, lowering the peak, reducing local curvature at the apex, and redistributing weight into the shoulders. This is a changed target mapping, not just an auxiliary reinterpretation, because the observed sigma at the same nominal E_cm differs between spread settings. A single parent parameter set cannot reproduce both conditions simultaneously: increasing intrinsic_width to mimic a broadened scan also alters the tails and the whole denominator structure in a way tied to the intrinsic propagator, whereas external smearing mainly suppresses and smooths the central feature through convolution. The effect is scientifically admissible and visible when the spread is comparable to the intrinsic width over the scanned window; if the spread is much smaller than the width everywhere, the refinement becomes weak and the parent is approximately recovered."
  },
